Jahshaka patch 0083 — the diffuse cone basis is a function of the normal.

MEDIA-only patch (one Hlms piece: Samples/Media/Hlms/Pbs/Any/Vct_piece_ps.any).
Studio stages its media from the SOURCE tree (cmake/IncludeOgre.cmake), so every
tree still has to re-run `irisgl/scripts/build-ogre.sh` to get it — no engine
COMPILE is needed beyond the staging, which is the whole difference from a
SOURCE patch. It OVERLAPS patches 0021, 0033, 0045, 0048, 0066 and 0070 in that
one file: a tree that already carries those hits `build-ogre.sh`'s "Upstream
changed the patched file" on a re-run, which is the documented per-patch
reverse-check blind spot and NOT an upstream change. The cure is the recorded
one — reset the submodule (`git -C irisgl/thirdparty/ogre-next checkout -- . &&
git -C irisgl/thirdparty/ogre-next clean -fd`) and re-run the script.

WHAT THE USER SEES (owner review 2026-09-18, screenshots 10 and 11): a hard
DIAGONAL across one flat face of a cube, corner to corner, with a stippled GRAIN
over it — a flat, uniformly lit, single-material quad shaded as two visibly
different triangles.

WHY. `applyVoxelConeTracing` traces six diffuse cones in TANGENT SPACE and needs
a frame to put them in. Upstream handed it the frame that happened to be in
scope:

  * where the datablock has a normal map (`normal_map`, which every tangented
    mesh in this tree generates) it is the MATERIAL's TBN — `normalize(
    inPs.tangent )` and the binormal built from it. That is a per-VERTEX
    attribute, authored for TEXTURING, and it has three properties that are all
    fine for its own job and all wrong for this one: it is INTERPOLATED, so it
    is a different function inside each of a quad's two triangles (they are
    built from different vertex triples) — the diagonal; it is interpolated PER
    PIXEL and renormalised, so it rotates continuously across a surface — the
    grain; and it follows the UV authoring, so the same box turned on its own
    axis gets a different frame — the same solid shades differently.
  * everywhere else it is `generateTbn`, whose tangent is `OGRE_ddx( pos )`, and
    whose own comment in this file says the result "isn't fully stable due to
    how DDX works and due to floating point precision".

Neither is a defect in ITS OWN job. The defect is that a cone DISTRIBUTION is
not a tangent-space quantity at all: nothing about where the six cones should
point depends on how the artist laid out the UVs. And the error is not small,
because the cone set is not azimuthally symmetric against an ANISOTROPIC voxel
field — the six directional volumes are sampled by three-axis blends, so
rotating the frame about the normal changes which volumes each cone reads.

MEASURED (lane CUBE-SHADE-1, 2026-09-21, a 2 m cube on the default ground at
Epic, 1920x1080, the editor's own grade):

  turning the cube 90 degrees about its own axis — the same solid, in the same
  place, with the same world geometry and the same face normal:

      before   92.9 % of the face's pixels move, worst 16/255, mean 4.8
      after     2.96 %                          , worst  1/255  (the dither)

  the high-frequency energy inside one triangle of the face (mean |p - the mean
  of its two horizontal neighbours|, in display codes):

      before   3.28        GI off (the floor)  0.51
      after    0.49        GI+AO off           0.54

  i.e. the grain is gone to the dither floor, and the diagonal with it.

WHAT THIS PATCH DOES. `buildConeBasis( geomNormal )` — Frisvad's orthonormal
basis (JGT 2012), continuous over the whole sphere but for the single direction
(0,0,-1), where the branch returns a fixed frame instead of dividing by zero.
The hairy-ball theorem says no continuous tangent field on the sphere exists, so
SOME direction has to be special; a single POINT is the best available, and it
was chosen over the branchless (Duff 2017) variant for exactly that reason —
Duff's sign flip puts the discontinuity on the whole great circle n.z = 0, which
in an axis-aligned scene contains the floor's normal and two of the four wall
normals. Measured side by side on spheres, cylinders and a torus the two agree
over 99.5 % of the frame; where they differ it is at their singular sets.

The material's own TBN is untouched and goes on doing its one job (turning a
tangent-space normal map into a world-space normal). `generateTbn` had exactly
one call site, which is this one, and goes with it.

--- a/Samples/Media/Hlms/Pbs/Any/Vct_piece_ps.any
+++ b/Samples/Media/Hlms/Pbs/Any/Vct_piece_ps.any
@@ -59,19 +59,43 @@
 		float travelled;
 	};
 
-	/// Generates a TBN matrix when no tangent information is present using
-	/// DDX information.
-	/// Should only we used as a fallback as the result isn't fully stable due to how DDX works
-	/// and due to floating point precision. It is possible for the TBN to change as you zoom-in
-	/// to the triangle.
-	INLINE float3x3 generateTbn( float3 geomNormal, float3 pos )
+	/// Jahshaka patch 0083: THE CONE BASIS IS A FUNCTION OF THE NORMAL AND OF
+	/// NOTHING ELSE. `buildConeBasis` returns an orthonormal frame whose third
+	/// axis is the geometric normal, built by Frisvad's method (JGT 2012): it is
+	/// continuous over the whole sphere except at the single direction
+	/// (0, 0, -1), where the branch below hands back a fixed frame rather than
+	/// dividing by zero.
+	///
+	/// The two frames this REPLACES were both unstable, and the instability is
+	/// visible because the diffuse cone set is not azimuthally symmetric against
+	/// an anisotropic voxel field: rotate the frame about the normal and the six
+	/// cones read different axis volumes.
+	///   * the material's TANGENT frame (what the normal_map permutations used):
+	///     a per-VERTEX attribute authored for texturing, interpolated from a
+	///     different vertex triple in each triangle of a quad, so one flat face
+	///     shaded as two triangles with a hard kink along its own diagonal, with
+	///     per-pixel grain across each, and the same box shaded differently after
+	///     being turned on its own axis.
+	///   * the DDX fallback (what the rest used), whose own comment above said it
+	///     "isn't fully stable due to how DDX works and due to floating point
+	///     precision".
+	INLINE float3x3 buildConeBasis( float3 geomNormal )
 	{
-		float3 tangentX = OGRE_ddx( pos.xyz ).xyz;
-		tangentX = normalize( tangentX );
-
-		float3 binormal = cross( geomNormal, tangentX ) ;
-		float3x3 TBN = buildFloat3x3( tangentX, binormal, geomNormal );
-		return TBN;
+		float3 tangentX;
+		float3 binormal;
+		if( geomNormal.z < -0.9999999 )
+		{
+			tangentX = float3( 0.0, -1.0, 0.0 );
+			binormal = float3( -1.0, 0.0, 0.0 );
+		}
+		else
+		{
+			float a = 1.0 / ( 1.0 + geomNormal.z );
+			float b = -geomNormal.x * geomNormal.y * a;
+			tangentX = float3( 1.0 - geomNormal.x * geomNormal.x * a, b, -geomNormal.x );
+			binormal = float3( b, 1.0 - geomNormal.y * geomNormal.y * a, -geomNormal.y );
+		}
+		return buildFloat3x3( tangentX, binormal, geomNormal );
 	}
 
 	@foreach( 2, n )
@@ -790,9 +814,12 @@
 @end
 
 @piece( applyVoxelConeTracing )
-	@property( !normal_map )
-		float3x3 TBN = generateTbn( pixelData.geomNormal, inPs.pos );
-	@end
+	// Jahshaka patch 0083: the cones get their own frame, the same one for every
+	// pixel of every surface that shares a normal. The outer scope's TBN (the
+	// material's, when it has a normal map) stays exactly what it is and keeps
+	// doing the one job it is for -- turning a tangent-space normal map into a
+	// world-space normal.
+	float3x3 jahConeBasis = buildConeBasis( float3( pixelData.geomNormal ) );
 
 	@property( use_parallax_correct_cubemaps )
 		float3 vctSpecPosVS = float3( 0, 0, 0 );
@@ -811,7 +838,7 @@
 	@property( jah_sky_env_probe )
 					 jahEscapeColour, jahEscapeWeight, jahConeWeight,
 	@end
-					 pixelData, inPs.pos, TBN,
+					 pixelData, inPs.pos, jahConeBasis,
 				@property( vct_ambient_hemisphere )
 					 ambientWD, ambientWS,
 				@end
